888 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 150 0 R
/T (The simple design assumes a single rollover whenever the current reading is less than the previous reading. It cannot distinguish a genuine rollover from a meter reset, data-entry mistake, or multiple missed cycles. It also uses one tariff for the whole billing period and does not yet account for taxes, subsidies, meter faults, or time-of-day usage. These cases require additional rules and records.)
/E (The simple design assumes a single rollover whenever the current reading is less than the previous reading. It cannot distinguish a genuine rollover from a meter reset, data-entry mistake, or multiple missed cycles. It also uses one tariff for the whole billing period and does not yet account for taxes, subsidies, meter faults, or time-of-day usage. These cases require additional rules and records.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
889 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 150 0 R
/T (The bill calculation for one household remains O\(1\) because it uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations. Processing n household bills takes O\(n\) time. A basic program can handle 800,000 bills if memory, input/output, and integer ranges are managed correctly. A production system should stream records or process batches, validate each record, log rejected records, and use checked wide-integer aggregation.)
/E (The bill calculation for one household remains O\(1\) because it uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations. Processing n household bills takes O\(n\) time. A basic program can handle 800,000 bills if memory, input/output, and integer ranges are managed correctly. A production system should stream records or process batches, validate each record, log rejected records, and use checked wide-integer aggregation.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
890 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 150 0 R
/T (Retain integer paise and wide integer totals, but modify the program to separate the tariff configuration from the source code. Use a validated configuration file for rates, explicit error handling, a rollover/reset check, and automated boundary tests. The recommendation should be supported by actual compiler runs and measured totals recorded in the submission.)
/E (Retain integer paise and wide integer totals, but modify the program to separate the tariff configuration from the source code. Use a validated configuration file for rates, explicit error handling, a rollover/reset check, and automated boundary tests. The recommendation should be supported by actual compiler runs and measured totals recorded in the submission.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
891 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 150 0 R
/T (If slab limits and rates are stored in arrays and processed in a loop, the tariff function takes O\(s\) time for s slabs and O\(s\) extra space for the tariff table. With only three slabs, fixed if-statements are simple and take O\(1\) time and O\(1\) space. The array approach is easier to revise and extend to many slabs. Reading t tariff values from a configuration file takes O\(t\) start-up time and O\(t\) storage, but avoids recompilation after each approved tariff change.)
/E (If slab limits and rates are stored in arrays and processed in a loop, the tariff function takes O\(s\) time for s slabs and O\(s\) extra space for the tariff table. With only three slabs, fixed if-statements are simple and take O\(1\) time and O\(1\) space. The array approach is easier to revise and extend to many slabs. Reading t tariff values from a configuration file takes O\(t\) start-up time and O\(t\) storage, but avoids recompilation after each approved tariff change.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
